well let's turn to labor now shall we  and find out what they made of that  interview with the chancellor we can  speak now to the shadow Chancellor  Rachel Reeves who joins us now from  Leeds thank you very much for being on  the program at this morning  um Jeremy Hunt telling me there that  everyone is going to be paying a little  bit more tax after next week do you  think he's got the right plan  well we'll see what he comes up with on  Thursday but I am determined to hold him  to account for having a fair tax system  and what I would like to see on Thursday  and what I would be doing if I was  Chancellor of the exchequer is closing  some of the tax loopholes which mean  today that some of the wealthiest in  society and some of the most biggest  businesses are not paying their fair  share of taxes that's why for example  when the when the Prime Minister flies  off to the G20 Summit today he should be  committing to closing some of those  loopholes that mean big Global  multinationals not paying their fair  share of tax there is already a global  agreement but we haven't legislated for  it here in the UK and it could bring in  7 billion pounds a year as well as  helping some of our smaller businesses  and High Street businesses as you know  Sophie I've been banging on about the  windfall tax on big energy companies  since the beginning of this year and the  Prime Minister when he was Chancellor  slow pedaled on that we still think that  you could raise an additional 50 billion  pounds through extending the windfall  tax by about two years by back dating it  to the beginning of January when those  windfalls those profits of War started  coming onto the balance sheets of the  energy companies by getting rid of some  of the loopholes that mean companies  like shell are not even paying any of  the government's energy profits Levy and  extending it to some of the electricity  generators so there are fair choices  that the chancellor could be making this  week rather than just putting all of  that burden on ordinary working people  who are already struggling with the  highest inflation that any of us have  experienced for 40 years and now risk  more of their money going in taxes  rather than having it for the weekly  food shop the higher mortgage payments  and everything else that is facing  people right now and I take your point  that labor was of course the first to  call for the windfall tax on energy  firms but it does look like according to  reports that the government is going to  be extending uh that uh next week Jeremy  Hunt was also talking about the cops let  me just say let me just  let me just say it on a win four tax I  hope the government does extend it but I  hope they do what labor are calling for  which is ensuring that the tax paid by  energy companies here is similar to what  it is in Norway and that we get rid of  those loopholes that mean for more  investment in fossil fuels those energy  companies can offset the wind forecast  that is not right companies should be  paying their fair share and that would  ease the pressure on the tax burden for  ordinary working people okay the  question that I was going to ask was  that Jeremy Hunt was also talking about  the cost of the energy support package  saying that with the rise in energy  bills it's going to be like funding a  second NHS so I just wonder what Labor's  plan is how long do you think the energy  price guarantee should last and is there  a point when actually it should be  targeted at the most vulnerable  pensioners those on low incomes rather  than just everybody  well when um Kia Stormer and me set out  our plans for the Autumn um winter  months and we did that in August at that  time the conservatives were all saying  um that they didn't believe in in  handouts they're constantly playing  catch up with what labor are putting  forward and of course the new Chancellor  Jeremy Hunt would reduce the support  that was available to people now look  we'll see where we are going into the  new year and where energy uh prices are  but labor have always been very clear  that the windfall tax on energy  companies should be used to try and keep  bills as low as possible for pensioners  for families and also for businesses and  you know I speak to many businesses  including here in Leeds and in fact I  was talking to one business owner  yesterday who said that she wasn't even  able to get a contract for energy so she  doesn't know from one week to the next  what she's paying because she's now just  on a floating a deal tracking Energy  prices that's incredibly difficult for  her and her business because energy  costs are a big part of it so we need  more certainty and Clarity from the  government you're not giving us what is  going to be available after after April  you're not giving the certainty in  Clarity either though are you because I  just asked you know firstly if you think  it should be targeted and secondly how  long it would last for under labor  well I I've made this commitment that we  would extend the uh windfall tax on  those big oil and gas companies and  we've always said that if you do that  there's more money to help people with  their gas and electricity uh bills and  the government have always slow pedaled  on the support that is needed it's why  also labor came up with a package for  some of our most energy intensive  Industries who are being put a  comparative disadvantage compared with  some of our International competitors  who are being supported and the  government has never come up with a  package to help them in September you  said on GMB austerity is not the right  approach we will oppose any cuts to  Public Services is that still your  position that you will oppose any cuts  to Public Services  public services are already on their  knees seven million people are waiting  for an NHS operation uh or or support  um you know if you look at our schools  our class sizes are increasing teachers  are increasingly having to fund at the  basics out of their own money and so I I  don't believe that austerity 2.0 after  the austerity that we've gone through  after the last 12 years it is the right  approach which is why I'm arguing for  two things both fairer choices on taxes  but also crucially a plan for growth  because one of the reasons that we are  in this mess we're in and why we have  been so uniquely exposed in Britain with  one of the lowest growths of any of the  oecd the major industrialized economies  is because we've just been languishing  in the league tables in growth over the  last decade so we need a growth plan  it's why I've set out Labor's green  Prosperity plan because I generally only  believe that Britain can be a global  leader in some of the industries of the  future from Green hydrogen to tidal  energy floating offshore winds carbon  capture and storage creating good jobs  in all parts of Britain helping keep  people's bills low and also boosting our  energy security it's why we're set out  our plans to make Britain the best place  to start and grow a business and  reforming business rates to help our  small businesses and our High Street  Thrive we need all of that because  that's what brings in the money to pay  for our public services and the problem  is now after 12 years of conservative  government growth is on the floor and  inflation is going through the roof I'm  going to jump in just because I've got a  couple more questions that I do want to  squeeze in before I have to let you go  and Jeremy Hunt was saying on a public  sector pay and on strikes if we gave  everyone inflation-proof pay Rises then  inflation would stay at nurses currently  striking they want an above inflation  pay rise of 17 percent do you think  that's affordable  I'm not going to pluck numbers out of  the air but it should be a number that's  on the table for the conservatives  well  it's up to the pay review bodies to work  with the unions but it is a badge of  shame that for the first time in their  history nurses are looking to go on  strike this didn't happen when labor was  in government because we valued and  respected Public Service workers  including those who work in our NHS  who've given so much to our country  these last few years we've got huge  recruitment challenges in the NHS  including amongst nurses at workload  issues issues of stress we've got nurses  that are going to food banks so we need  to support our NHS workers including our  nurses and that means fair but  affordable pay Rises I'm not going to  cut into the uh the pay review  negotiations that are going uh on but  the Secretary of State for health should  meet with the nurses and the nursing  unions and work out a deal and and show  some respect to these crucial Public  Service workers  I've just got one more question to  squeeze in  um over the summer you were pictured uh  chatting at to Rishi sunak at a  Bloomberg a drinks event it was shortly  before he was made prime minister  what were we talking about  uh well we were a reception and uh I  shadowed a richly sooner for about a  year and a half uh we've talked about a  number of things but it also  particularly actually about our children  we both got young children we were  talking about the influence that they  have on our politics  um I was also you know commiserating  with him on on losing the leadership  contest uh he did lose that contest to  Liz truss is now prime minister but he's  not willing to face the people there  should be a general election because he  has no mandates he was not chosen by  either his members and certainly not by  the country there's no mandate for the  cuts and the tax increases that they are  putting through now the people should  have a choice about who their prime  minister and who their Chancellor is  that will only come through a general  election and that's why we desperately  need one to have a mandate for the  decisions that are being made in our  name uh thank you very much indeed  Rachel Reeves the safe space I feel talk  about children in the political Arena  Arena

